; An `open` whose `gnucash-name` has a part that names nothing.
;
; Two shapes, one rule: the name is empty outright, and the name ends in the
; separator (`Assets:Bank:`), which is a part as empty as the whole.
;
; The key is required and this file has it, so the required-metadata check
; passed. Downstream, looking an empty path up answers with the root account —
; `find_account` reads `''` as "the tree itself" — and the account creation
; took that for "already there" and skipped, so the book never gained the
; account, the run still counted it, and `✓ Import successful / Accounts: 3`
; was printed over a book holding two. A later posting on it then failed with
; a message about an account mapping, which is not where the fault was.
;
; The trailing separator went the other way and made an account: a child with
; no name at all under Assets:Bank, which GnuCash shows as an empty row and
; every export writes back as the same typo.
;
; What this fixture produces: a refusal naming the account whose name is
; empty.
;
; Verified by tests/integration/test_beancount_reads_what_a_person_writes.py.
2024-01-01 commodity CAD
    gnucash-mnemonic: "CAD"
    gnucash-namespace: "CURRENCY"
    gnucash-fullname: "Canadian Dollar"
    gnucash-fraction: "100"
2024-01-01 open Assets CAD
    gnucash-name: "Assets"
    gnucash-guid: "1111111111111111111111111111111a"
    gnucash-type: "Asset"
    gnucash-placeholder: "False"
2024-01-01 open Assets:Bank CAD
    gnucash-name: ""
    gnucash-guid: "1111111111111111111111111111111b"
    gnucash-type: "Bank"
    gnucash-placeholder: "False"
2024-01-01 open Expenses CAD
    gnucash-name: "Expenses"
    gnucash-guid: "1111111111111111111111111111111c"
    gnucash-type: "Expense"
    gnucash-placeholder: "False"

2024-02-01 * "Payee" "Lunch"
    gnucash-guid: "2222222222222222222222222222222a"
  Expenses 50.00 CAD
  Assets:Bank -50.00 CAD
